traights and arcs, ego plus one IDM vehicle.\n\nThe first of the two envs whose ego is a plain ``Vehicle``, so its four tyres are drawn and\n``ego_steering`` is a state dimension. See ``_add_tires`` for the nested pivot; nothing else here\ndiffers from the rest of the family.", "has_step": false, "render_overridden": true, "backends": ["raster2d", "scene"]}, "Parking": {"name": "parking", "reference_id": "parking-v0", "fidelity_tier": "parity", "render_decoder": "renderer", "visual_fidelity": {}, "visual_note": "", "parity_interior_min": 1.0, "parity_note": "", "state_dim": 0, "default_size": 300, "state_names": [], "step_reference_exact": null, "step_reference_control": null, "step_reference_gate": null, "class": "Parking", "module": "stable_envs.envs.highway", "doc": "parking-v0: a 14-bay lot, one ego, a goal landmark and four walls.\n\nThree things are specific to this env and each is measured rather than assumed:\n\n  * its ``scaling`` is 7, so ``pix(STRIPE_WIDTH) = 2`` and the markings take the even-width branch\n    of ``_line_geom`` -- the same branch intersection-v0 needed;\n  * the GOAL is a ``Landmark`` whose bay is drawn at random each episode, so its pose is part of\n    the state rather than a class constant, and it is drawn BLUE (``RoadObjectGraphics.get_color``)\n    rather than the obstacles' yellow. The 28 poses it takes are the bay centres, which is what\n    ``object_spawn`` draws from;\n  * the four walls are ``Obstacle``\\ s 70 m and 42 m long -- 490 px and 294 px at this scaling --\n    and ``RoadObjectGraphics.display``, unlike ``VehicleGraphics.display``, does NOT test\n    ``is_visible``. ``_add_box(cull=False)`` is that difference. Measured on 64 states with the ego\n    driven to the edges of the lot, culling road objects the way vehicles are culled costs 7 px a\n    frame -- small, because a wall leaves the frame along its 7 px THICKNESS and the visibility\n    margin is 50 px, so it is already invisible by the time its centre is culled. It is followed\n    because it is the reference's rule, not because the difference is large.\n\n**``crashed`` is not in this env's state, and that is a measurement rather than an omission.**\n``ParkingEnv._create_vehicles`` sets ``vehicle.color = VehicleGraphics.EGO_COLOR``, and\n``get_color`` returns ``vehicle.color`` BEFORE it ever tests ``crashed`` -- so the ego is green\nwhether or not it has crashed, there is no second vehicle to paint red, and both the state\ndimension and the ``crashed.color`` knob would be dead weight. ``reference_set_state`` asserts the\npinned colour is still there, so a package that stopped pinning it fails loudly instead of\nrendering a green car where the reference draws a red one. The same reasoning removes ``traffic``:\n``vehicles_count`` is 0, so there is no traffic to colour.", "has_step": false, "render_overridden": true, "backends": ["raster2d", "scene"]}, "Highway": {"name": "highway", "reference_id": "highway-v0", "fidelity_tier": "parity", "render_decoder": "renderer", "visual_fidelity": {}, "visual_note": "", "parity_interior_min": 1.0, "parity_note": "", "state_dim": 0, "default_size": 150, "state_names": [], "step_reference_exact": null, "step_reference_control": null, "step_reference_gate": null, "class": "Highway", "module": "stable_envs.envs.highway", "doc": "highway-v0: four straight lanes, ego plus traffic, camera locked to the ego.", "has_step": false, "render_overridden": true, "backends": ["raster2d", "scene"]}, "Merge": {"name": "merge", "reference_id": "merge-v0", "fidelity_tier": "parity", "render_decoder": "renderer", "visual_fidelity": {}, "visual_note": "", "parity_interior_min": 1.0, "parity_note": "", "state_dim": 0, "default_size": 150, "state_names": [], "step_reference_exact": null, "step_reference_control": null, "step_reference_gate": null, "class": "Merge", "module": "stable_envs.envs.highway", "doc": "merge-v0: a two-lane highway with a sinusoidal on-ramp and a yellow obstacle at its end.", "has_step": false, "render_overridden": true, "backends": ["raster2d", "scene"]}, "Roundabout": {"name": "roundabout", "reference_id": "roundabout-v0", "fidelity_tier": "parity", "render_decoder": "renderer", "visual_fidelity": {}, "visual_note": "", "parity_interior_min": 1.0, "parity_note": "", "state_dim": 0, "default_size": 600, "state_names": [], "step_reference_exact": null, "step_reference_control": null, "step_reference_gate": null, "class": "Roundabout", "module": "stable_envs.envs.highway", "doc": "roundabout-v0: a two-ring roundabout with four access branches, ego plus four vehicles.\n\nAt 604 primitives this is the largest scene in the repo, and it was unrenderable until the\nrasterizer stopped unrolling its primitive list: ``_scene_kernel`` used ``tl.static_range``, which\nmade the primitive count a compile-time constant, and ptxas aborted the process above ~340. The\nunroll is gone (``raster2d: runtime primitive loop``) and this env is the reason it went; the\nmeasured ceiling is kept in ``docs/research/highway.md`` because it will come back if anyone\nreintroduces unrolling.\n\nIts markings are the one place a capsule and a Bresenham line genuinely differ: the arcs are drawn\nby the reference as chains of short 1 px lines at arbitrary angles, and those account for the\nremaining ~0.06% of pixels. All of them are 1 px wide, so none is an interior pixel.", "has_step": false, "render_overridden": true, "backends": ["raster2d", "scene"]}, "Intersection": {"name": "intersection", "reference_id": "intersection-v0", "fidelity_tier": "parity", "render_decoder": "renderer", "visual_fidelity": {}, "visual_note": "", "parity_interior_min": 1.0, "parity_note": "", "state_dim": 0, "default_size": 600, "state_names": [], "step_reference_exact": null, "step_reference_control": null, "step_reference_gate": null, "class": "Intersection", "module": "stable_envs.envs.highway", "doc": "intersection-v0: a four-way junction, ego approaching from the south, traffic crossing.\n\nTwo things make this one different from the rest of the family. Its ``scaling`` is ``5.5 * 1.3``,\nso ``pix(STRIPE_WIDTH) = 2`` and the markings leave pygame's Bresenham path \u2014 see ``_line_geom``\nfor the width-2 rule and what it cost to get wrong. And the ego is appended to ``road.vehicles``\nLAST rather than first, so it paints on top of the traffic; ``EGO_INDEX`` says so, because at a\njunction vehicles overlap by design and the order is the difference between the ego being visible\nand being buried.\n\nThe reference decides its cast at random \u2014 ``_spawn_vehicle`` draws against a probability and\n``_clear_vehicles`` deletes vehicles that leave \u2014 so the number of vehicles after ``reset`` is not\na property of the env id. ``reference_set_state`` rebuilds ``road.vehicles`` outright, which is\nwhat makes a fixed-width state well defined here at all.", "has_step": false, "render_overridden": true, "backends": ["raster2d", "scene"]}, "PointMazeUMaze": {"name": "pointmaze-umaze", "reference_id": "PointMaze_UMaze-v3", "fidelity_tier": "parity", "render_decoder": "renderer", "visual_fidelity": {}, "visual_note": "", "parity_interior_min": 1.0, "parity_note": "MOVING-PRIMITIVE PARITY (supersample 2, against MuJoCo at its default offsamples=4). The baked plate carries every static pixel, so the residual is confined to the two spheres, which together cover 0.52% of a 224x224 frame. Whole-frame agreement is 99.53% of pixels exact at mean 0.024/255; on the pixels the two sites actually touch it is 41-47% within 1/255 at mean 4.4/255. Two mechanisms, both measured and neither closable by fitting: (1) MuJoCo's 4x multisampling at the silhouette of a 12-pixel sphere, which an analytic caster reproduces only as coverage-free hard edges -- the same effect ogbench.py records for its agent; and (2) the fixed-function shading of a saturated primary colour, where the target's red channel clips at 1.0 and carries no information to fit against. A grid search over (ambient, diffuse, specular, exponent) around the model's declared headlight moved within-1 agreement from 0.410 to 0.468 and whole-frame exactness from 0.995326 to 0.995455, i.e. by less than the multisampling costs, so the DECLARED headlight constants are used rather than fitted ones. INTERIOR agreement, which is what parity_interior_min measures, is 1.000000: the interior mask keeps only pixels whose 3x3 neighbourhood in the reference is one flat colour, and a shaded 12-pixel sphere has none.", "state_dim": 4, "default_size": 224, "state_names": ["ball_x", "ball_y", "goal_x", "goal_y"], "step_reference_exact": null, "step_reference_control": null, "step_reference_gate": null, "class": "PointMazeUMaze", "module": "stable_envs.envs.maze2d", "doc": "gymnasium-robotics PointMaze_UMaze-v3 (D4RL maze2d-umaze). State is the ball's (x, y) and the goal's (x, y); the Dense and Diverse variants of this id differ only in reward and goal sampling and share this renderer.", "has_step": false, "render_overridden": true, "backends": ["raycast3d"]}, "PointMazeOpen": {"name": "pointmaze-open", "reference_id": "PointMaze_Open-v3", "fidelity_tier": "parity", "render_decoder": "renderer", "visual_fidelity": {}, "visual_note": "", "parity_interior_min": 1.0, "parity_note": "MOVING-PRIMITIVE PARITY (supersample 2, against MuJoCo at its default offsamples=4). The baked plate carries every static pixel, so the residual is confined to the two spheres, which together cover 0.52% of a 224x224 frame. Whole-frame agreement is 99.53% of pixels exact at mean 0.024/255; on the pixels the two sites actually touch it is 41-47% within 1/255 at mean 4.4/255. Two mechanisms, both measured and neither closable by fitting: (1) MuJoCo's 4x multisampling at the silhouette of a 12-pixel sphere, which an analytic caster reproduces only as coverage-free hard edges -- the same effect ogbench.py records for its agent; and (2) the fixed-function shading of a saturated primary colour, where the target's red channel clips at 1