nce's own rasterization and non-mover pixels are 99.76-99.93% bit-identical; the whole residual is inside the moving primitives, which are 0.3-4.4% of the frame. There the shader reaches 3-32% of pixels exact and 37-67% within 1/255 (mean 2.5-7.7/255), and on mover pixels eroded by one \u2014 so coverage is not in play \u2014 6-34% exact and 53-72% within 1/255 (mean 2.2-3.9/255). THOSE MOVER NUMBERS ARE THE PRICE OF ss=1 AND ARE STATED AS SUCH: at supersample 2 the identical protocol gives 16-35% / 64-78% (mean 2.0-3.1) and 20-37% / 67-88% (mean 1.3-2.0). None of it reaches an assertion \u2014 the interior metric excludes a shaded capsule almost everywhere, and whole-frame exactness and mean keep their margins (worst 0.9670 against a 0.90 floor, worst mean 0.134 against a 4.0 ceiling, over 201 draws) \u2014 but a caller who is scored on mover pixels should pass supersample=2, which is still there. Against a NON-multisampled reference the same shader reaches 71-90% within 1/255 (mean 1.27-1.86), which puts a large part of the gap in MSAA sample positions rather than in shading. The rest is MuJoCo shading curved primitives PER VERTEX over a tessellation while this is a per-pixel evaluation, and that diagnosis is measured rather than asserted: a least-squares fit of (ambient, diffuse, specular, exponent) on 18,231 mover-interior pixels returns ambient 0.103 and diffuse 0.388 against the nominal 0.1 and 0.4 \u2014 the diffuse law is right \u2014 and moves only the highlight, to a broader and weaker lobe (0.177 at exponent 32 against 0.25 at 64), which is what interpolating a per-pixel highlight across a triangle looks like. Adopting that lobe was tried at four settings and REFUTED as a family-wide constant: it improves InvertedDoublePendulum (within-1 0.872 -> 0.885) and InvertedPendulum (0.728 -> 0.736) and makes Reacher (0.685 -> 0.668) and Pusher (0.729 -> 0.677) worse, so the documented law is kept.", "state_dim": 0, "default_size": 240, "state_names": [], "step_reference_exact": null, "step_reference_control": null, "step_reference_gate": null, "class": "MujocoReacher", "module": "stable_envs.envs.mujoco_locomotion", "doc": "Gymnasium Reacher-v5. State is qpos; the frame is a measured pure function of it, and the camera gymnasium freezes at the first render is carried in the style.", "has_step": false, "render_overridden": true, "backends": ["nvdiff", "raycast3d"]}, "MujocoPusher": {"name": "mujoco-pusher", "reference_id": "gymnasium/Pusher-v5", "fidelity_tier": "parity", "render_decoder": "renderer", "visual_fidelity": {}, "visual_note": "", "parity_interior_min": 0.99999, "parity_note": "MOVER-PIXEL PARITY (supersample 1, against gymnasium at MuJoCo's default offsamples=4, 240 px, 32 states per env). The plate is baked through MuJoCo itself, so every static pixel is the reference's own rasterization and non-mover pixels are 99.76-99.93% bit-identical; the whole residual is inside the moving primitives, which are 0.3-4.4% of the frame. There the shader reaches 3-32% of pixels exact and 37-67% within 1/255 (mean 2.5-7.7/255), and on mover pixels eroded by one \u2014 so coverage is not in play \u2014 6-34% exact and 53-72% within 1/255 (mean 2.2-3.9/255). THOSE MOVER NUMBERS ARE THE PRICE OF ss=1 AND ARE STATED AS SUCH: at supersample 2 the identical protocol gives 16-35% / 64-78% (mean 2.0-3.1) and 20-37% / 67-88% (mean 1.3-2.0). None of it reaches an assertion \u2014 the interior metric excludes a shaded capsule almost everywhere, and whole-frame exactness and mean keep their margins (worst 0.9670 against a 0.90 floor, worst mean 0.134 against a 4.0 ceiling, over 201 draws) \u2014 but a caller who is scored on mover pixels should pass supersample=2, which is still there. Against a NON-multisampled reference the same shader reaches 71-90% within 1/255 (mean 1.27-1.86), which puts a large part of the gap in MSAA sample positions rather than in shading. The rest is MuJoCo shading curved primitives PER VERTEX over a tessellation while this is a per-pixel evaluation, and that diagnosis is measured rather than asserted: a least-squares fit of (ambient, diffuse, specular, exponent) on 18,231 mover-interior pixels returns ambient 0.103 and diffuse 0.388 against the nominal 0.1 and 0.4 \u2014 the diffuse law is right \u2014 and moves only the highlight, to a broader and weaker lobe (0.177 at exponent 32 against 0.25 at 64), which is what interpolating a per-pixel highlight across a triangle looks like. Adopting that lobe was tried at four settings and REFUTED as a family-wide constant: it improves InvertedDoublePendulum (within-1 0.872 -> 0.885) and InvertedPendulum (0.728 -> 0.736) and makes Reacher (0.685 -> 0.668) and Pusher (0.729 -> 0.677) worse, so the documented law is kept. INTERIOR: 8 disagreeing interior pixels out of 608,783,153 over 201 seeded draws of 64 states at the shipped supersample 1 \u2014 worst single draw 0.9999997, and most draws are exact \u2014 every one off by exactly 1/255 in a single channel. The cause is a one-ulp difference in float32 ``exp`` between torch's CPU implementation and this one, which reaches a pixel only where the specular term lands exactly on a rounding boundary \u2014 the same mechanism as InvertedPendulum above. Supersampling makes it WORSE, which is the clearest evidence available that it is a rounding boundary and not coverage: the same 201 draws at supersample 2 give 30 bad pixels against 8, because a blended pixel has more ways to land on a boundary than a covered one. The floor is set to 0.99999, which is 33x inside the measured worst draw. This env once declared 1.0 on the strength of a single unseeded draw that happened to come back clean; the floor is set from a draw COUNT, and that rule is why the supersample change was verified over 201 draws rather than over one large evaluation.", "state_dim": 0, "default_size": 240, "state_names": [], "step_reference_exact": null, "step_reference_control": null, "step_reference_gate": null, "class": "