asured rather than asserted: a least-squares fit of (ambient, diffuse, specular, exponent) on 18,231 mover-interior pixels returns ambient 0.103 and diffuse 0.388 against the nominal 0.1 and 0.4 \u2014 the diffuse law is right \u2014 and moves only the highlight, to a broader and weaker lobe (0.177 at exponent 32 against 0.25 at 64), which is what interpolating a per-pixel highlight across a triangle looks like. Adopting that lobe was tried at four settings and REFUTED as a family-wide constant: it improves InvertedDoublePendulum (within-1 0.872 -> 0.885) and InvertedPendulum (0.728 -> 0.736) and makes Reacher (0.685 -> 0.668) and Pusher (0.729 -> 0.677) worse, so the documented law is kept. INTERIOR: 164 disagreeing interior pixels out of 673,455,908 over 201 seeded draws of 64 states at the shipped supersample 1, all inside the cart or pole capsule and all off by exactly 1/255; the count is IDENTICAL at supersample 2 and unchanged at supersample 4, so it is the per-vertex/per-pixel shading difference above and not a coverage rule. The worst single draw is 0.9999988, and the floor is set to 0.99999, which is 8.4x inside it.", "state_dim": 0, "default_size": 240, "state_names": [], "step_reference_exact": null, "step_reference_control": null, "step_reference_gate": null, "class": "MujocoInvertedPendulum", "module": "stable_envs.envs.mujoco_locomotion", "doc": "Gymnasium InvertedPendulum-v5. State is qpos; the frame is a measured pure function of it, and the camera gymnasium freezes at the first render is carried in the style.", "has_step": false, "render_overridden": true, "backends": ["nvdiff", "raycast3d"]}, "MujocoInvertedDoublePendulum": {"name": "mujoco-inverted-double-pendulum", "reference_id": "gymnasium/InvertedDoublePendulum-v5", "fidelity_tier": "parity", "render_decoder": "renderer", "visual_fidelity": {}, "visual_note": "", "parity_interior_min": 1.0, "parity_note": "MOVER-PIXEL PARITY (supersample 1, against gymnasium at MuJoCo's default offsamples=4, 240 px, 32 states per env). The plate is baked through MuJoCo itself, so every static pixel is the reference's own rasterization and non-mover pixels are 99.76-99.93% bit-identical; the whole residual is inside the moving primitives, which are 0.3-4.4% of the frame. There the shader reaches 3-32% of pixels exact and 37-67% within 1/255 (mean 2.5-7.7/255), and on mover pixels eroded by one \u2014 so coverage is not in play \u2014 6-34% exact and 53-72% within 1/255 (mean 2.2-3.9/255). THOSE MOVER NUMBERS ARE THE PRICE OF ss=1 AND ARE STATED AS SUCH: at supersample 2 the identical protocol gives 16-35% / 64-78% (mean 2.0-3.1) and 20-37% / 67-88% (mean 1.3-2.0). None of it reaches an assertion \u2014 the interior metric excludes a shaded capsule almost everywhere, and whole-frame exactness and mean keep their margins (worst 0.9670 against a 0.90 floor, worst mean 0.134 against a 4.0 ceiling, over 201 draws) \u2014 but a caller who is scored on mover pixels should pass supersample=2, which is still there. Against a NON-multisampled reference the same shader reaches 71-90% within 1/255 (mean 1.27-1.86), which puts a large part of the gap in MSAA sample positions rather than in shading. The rest is MuJoCo shading curved primitives PER VERTEX over a tessellation while this is a per-pixel evaluation, and that diagnosis is measured rather than asserted: a least-squares fit of (ambient, diffuse, specular, exponent) on 18,231 mover-interior pixels returns ambient 0.103 and diffuse 0.388 against the nominal 0.1 and 0.4 \u2014 the diffuse law is right \u2014 and moves only the highlight, to a broader and weaker lobe (0.177 at exponent 32 against 0.25 at 64), which is what interpolating a per-pixel highlight across a triangle looks like. Adopting that lobe was tried at four settings and REFUTED as a family-wide constant: it improves InvertedDoublePendulum (within-1 0.872 -> 0.885) and InvertedPendulum (0.728 -> 0.736) and makes Reacher (0.685 -> 0.668) and Pusher (0.729 -> 0.677) worse, so the documented law is kept.", "state_dim": 0, "default_size": 240, "state_names": [], "step_reference_exact": null, "step_reference_control": null, "step_reference_gate": null, "class": "MujocoInvertedDoublePendulum", "module": "stable_envs.envs.mujoco_locomotion", "doc": "Gymnasium InvertedDoublePendulum-v5. State is qpos; the frame is a measured pure function of it, and the camera gymnasium freezes at the first render is carried in the style.", "has_step": false, "render_overridden": true, "backends": ["nvdiff", "raycast3d"]}, "OGBCube": {"name": "ogb-cube", "reference_id": "swm/OGBCube-v0", "fidelity_tier": "visual", "render_decoder": "renderer", "visual_fidelity": {"mean_abs": 3.309378, "within8_frac": 0.91581, "ssim": 0.919219, "interior_exact_frac": 0.9578, "interior_frac": 0.412552, "all_exact_frac": 0.870012, "draw_spread": {"mean_abs": [3.265277, 3.309378], "ssim": [0.919219, 0.926569], "within8_frac": [0.91581, 0.917723]}, "mean_abs_max": 4.3, "within8_min": 0.85, "ssim_min": 0.85, "measured_at": "mujoco 3.12.0 / ogbench 1.2.1 reference; 224 px, camera front_pixels, 8 independent draws of 64 states from the env's own pool (reset + 0-25 random actions) on an RTX 3090 under MUJOCO_GL=egl, against swm's own render(); the declared value is the WORST of the eight and draw_spread carries the range, for mean_abs/within8_frac/ssim AND for interior_exact_frac, all_exact_frac and interior_frac. MEASURED THROUGH THE CHECK THAT GRADES IT, `check_visual_fidelity`, and one of the eight draws IS the draw the check takes -- it calls `sample_states(n)` with no rng, which is seed 0, the same fixed draw every time. The previous declaration was measured by a probe that averaged |diff| over the colour channels while `pixel_agreement` averages the per-pixel channel MAX, so it read 0.89/255 low and no widening rule could have covered the gap. The frame is a baked MuJoCo plate composited with MJWarp movers through the mover segmentation LUT, with gl_rounding and the shared out-of-range-spot correction applied. job 6433493 on gpu2109.oscar.ccv.brown.edu", "reference_versions": {"mujoco": "3.12.0", "ogbench": "1.2.1"}, "measured_with": {"mujoco_warp": "3.12.0"}}, "visual_note": "THREE declared residuals, all attributed by measurement before this class was written, and all structural to mujoco_warp rather than to this scene. (1) THE `global` DIRECTIONAL LIGHT is the largest remaining term: neutralising it on both sides moves interior exactness 0.347 -> 0.702. It is the castshadow light, and turning shadows OFF makes agreement worse, so the disagreement is in the light and not in its shadow. (2) THE SPOT'S DISTANCE ATTENUATION: after the shared out-of-range-cutoff correction, removing the spot entirely still adds +0.113, because MJWarp takes attenuation = 1/(1,d,d^2).(1,0,0) = 1 and MuJoCo evidently does something else with the same light_attenuation. (3) THE HEADLIGHT IS A MOVER-PIXEL TERM: interior barely moves (+0.001) while mover exactness goes 0.757 -> 0.896, which is what a light along the camera axis does. A fourth thing is NOT a residual and was checked: ambient. Zeroing it makes agreement WORSE, so MJWarp already agrees with MuJoCo about it. ALSO DECLARED, AND SEPARATE FROM THE SHADING: the translucent goal marker `target_object_0` renders at alpha 0.2 and mujoco_warp's shader reads only color[0..2], so it has no alpha path and would paint the marker opaque. Measured share of the frame: 0.61% (Cube) / 0.15% (Scene), against the 1.2-1.5% Fetch already declares for the same mechanism. WHERE THE RESIDUAL IS NOT: the floor. It is the baked plate, composited in wherever the mover LUT says MJWarp did not hit a mover, and the measured result is interior-exact 0.958 (Cube) / 0.922 (Scene) and whole-frame 0.872 / 0.775 over three independent 64-state draws. An earlier version of this cell masked with `seg >= 0` -- true wherever MJWarp hit ANYTHING, floor included -- so the plate reached only the sky and the same scene scored 0.351 interior-exact. That composite bug was worth more than every lighting term above combined.", "parity_interior_min": null, "parity_note": "", "state_dim": 0, "default_size": 224, "state_names": [], "step_reference_exact": null, "step_reference_control": null, "step_reference_gate": null, "class": "OGBCube", "module": "stable_envs.envs.ogb_manip", "doc": "OGBench manipulation Cube (swm/OGBCube-v0), fidelity_tier='visual'.\n\nThe frame is a MuJoCo-baked static plate composited with mujoco_warp's batch ray-caster for the movers, at swm's registered 224 px from the model camera 'front_pixels'. State is (qpos, mocap_pos), 24 dimensions. qvel is excluded because it reaches no pixel and mocap_quat because it is the identity everywhere in the registered distribution.\n\nTHIS IS A VISUAL TIER CELL, NOT A PARITY ONE. Three lighting terms in mujoco_warp disagree with MuJoCo and are declared in visual_fidelity and visual_note rather than chased: the global directional light, the spot's distance attenuation, and the headlight on mover pixels. A fourth, the out-of-range spot cutoff, WAS fixed and now lives in the shared render/mjwarp.py path. The translucent goal marker is a fifth, separate residual: mujoco_warp has no alpha path.\n\nThere is NO CPU backend: device='cpu' raises rather than silently using the GPU (CPU3D-OGB-1).", "has_step": false, "render_overridden": true, "backends": ["mjwarp"]}, "OGBScene": {"name": "ogb-scene", "reference_id": "swm/OGBScene-v0", "fidelity_tier": "visual", "render_decoder": "renderer", "visual_fidelity": {"mean_abs": 6.691589, "within8_frac": 0.855483, "ssim": 0.892792, "interior_exact_frac": 0.912032, "interior_frac": 0.278728, "all_exact_frac": 0.773417, "draw_spread": {"mean_abs": [6.493273, 6.691589], "ssim": [0.892792, 0.898809], "within8_frac": [0.855483, 0.860345]}, "mean_abs_max": 8.7, "within8_min": 0.8, "ssim_min": 0.85, "measured_at": "mujoco 3.12.0 / ogbench 1.2.1 reference; 200 px, camera front_pixels, 8 independent draws of 64 states from the env's own pool (reset + 0-25 random actions) on an RTX 3090 under MUJOCO_GL=egl, against swm's own render(); the declared value is the WORST of the eight and draw_spread carries the range, for mean_abs/within8_frac/ssim AND for interior_exact_frac, all_exact_frac and interior_frac. MEASURED THROUGH THE CHECK THAT GRADES IT, `check_visual_fidelity`, and one of the eight draws IS the draw the check takes -- it calls `sample_states(n)` with no rng, which is seed 0, the same fixed draw every time. The previous declaration was measured by a probe that averaged |diff| over the colour channels while `pixel_agreement` averages the per-pixel channel MAX, so it read 0.89/255 low and no widening rule could have covered the gap. The frame is a baked MuJoCo plate composited with MJWarp movers through the mover segmentation LUT, with gl_rounding and the shared out-of-range-spot correction applied. job 6433493 on gpu2109.oscar.ccv.brown.edu", "reference_versions": {"mujoco": "3.12.0", "ogbench": "1.2.1"}, "measured_with": {"mujoco_warp": "3.12.0"}}, "visual_note": "THREE declared residuals, all attributed by measurement before this class was written, and all structural to mujoco_warp rather than to this scene. (1) THE `global` DIRECTIONAL LIGHT is the largest remaining term: neutralising it on both sides moves interior exactness 0.347 -> 0.702. It is the castshadow light, and turning shadows OFF makes agreement worse, so the disagreement is in the light and not in its shadow. (2) THE SPOT'S DISTANCE ATTENUATION: after the shared out-of-range-cutoff correction, removing the spot entirely still adds +0.113, because MJWarp takes attenuation = 1/(1,d,d^2).(1,0,0) = 1 and MuJoCo evidently does something else with the same light_attenuation. (3) THE HEADLIGHT IS A MOVER-PIXEL TERM: interior barely moves (+0.001) while mover exactness goes 0.757 -> 0.896, which is what a light along the camera axis does. A fourth thing is NOT a residual and was checked: ambient. Zeroing it makes agreement WORSE, so MJWarp already agrees with MuJoCo about it. ALSO DECLARED, AND SEPARATE FROM THE SHADING: the translucent goal marker `target_object_0` renders at alpha 0.2 and mujoco_warp's shader reads only color[0..2], so it has no alpha path and would paint the marker opaque. Measured share of the frame: 0.61% (Cube) / 0.15% (Scene), against the 1.2-1.5% Fetch already declares for the same mechanism. WHERE THE RESIDUAL IS NOT: the floor. It is the baked plate, composited in wherever the mover LUT says MJWarp did not hit a mover, and the measured result is interior-exact 0.958 (Cube) / 0.922 (Scene) and whole-frame 0.872 / 0.775 over three independent 64-state draws. An earlier version of this cell masked with `seg >= 0` -- true wherever MJWarp hit ANYTHING, floor included -- so the plate reached only the sky and the same scene scored 0.351 interior-exact. That composite bug was worth more than every lighting term above combined. SCENE ONLY, A FOURTH DECLARED RESIDUAL: the material `btn_metal` wears a 512x512 CUBE map on two UV-less button meshes (`stopbuttonrim`, geoms //unnamed_geom_8 and //unnamed_geom_20), and mujoco_warp <= 3.12.0 indexes `mesh_texcoord` unconditionally in its MESH branch, so it dies with an illegal memory access before the first frame rather than drawing the scene badly. The texture is baked to its mean colour (0.523, 0.516, 0.512) through the shared `bake_unsamplable_textures`. MEASURED cost, MuJoCo against MuJoCo over 8 states at 200 px so no MJWarp term is mixed in: the two button geoms are 36 px/frame (0.090% of the frame), the bake changes 55.5 px/frame (0.139%), max 37/255, mean 0.023/255 over the frame, and interior_exact against the official frame is 1.00000 -- every changed pixel fails the 3x3-flat test, so the residual is invisible to the interior metric. For scale, the alpha note Fetch already declares is 1.2-1.5% of pixels. THE ZERO-PIXEL ALTERNATIVE EXISTS AND IS NOT TAKEN HERE: upstream mujoco_warp guards that branch from 3.13.0 (58d8c77, PR #1608), so a venv bump removes the need for this bake entirely -- to be measured, not assumed, since 3.13.0 moves more than one thing.", "parity_interior_min": null, "parity_note": "", "state_dim": 0, "default_size": 200, "state_names": [], "step_reference_exact": null, "step_reference_control": null, "step_reference_gate": null, "class": "OGBScene", "module": "stable_envs.envs.ogb_manip", "doc": "OGBench manipulation Scene (swm/OGBScene-v0), fidelity_tier='visual'.\n\nThe frame is a MuJoCo-baked static plate composited with mujoco_warp's batch ray-caster for the movers, at swm's registered 200 px from the model camera 'front_pixels'. State is (qpos, mocap_pos, button bits), 30 dimensions. qvel is excluded because it reaches no pixel and mocap_quat because it is the identity everywhere in the registered distribution.\n\nTHIS IS A VISUAL TIER CELL, NOT A PARITY ONE. Three lighting terms in mujoco_warp disagree with MuJoCo and are declared in visual_fidelity and visual_note rather than chased: the global directional light, the spot's distance attenuation, and the headlight on mover pixels. A fourth, the out-of-range spot cutoff, WAS fixed and now lives in the shared render/mjwarp.py path. The translucent goal marker is a fifth, separate residual: mujoco_warp has no alpha path.\n\nThere is NO CPU backend: device='cpu' raises rather than silently using the GPU (CPU3D-OGB-1).", "has_step": false, "render_overridden": true, "backends": ["mjwarp"]}, "PointmazeMediumNavigate": {"name": "pointmaze-medium-navigate", "reference_id": "ogbench/pointmaze-medium-v0", "fidelity_tier": "parity", "render_decoder": "renderer", "visual_fidelity": {}, "visual_note": "", "parity_interior_min": 1.0, "parity_note": "AGENT-PIXEL PARITY (supersample 2, against MuJoCo at its default offsamples=4, 25 cells x 64 states). Every disagreement is inside the agent's own silhouette: the baked plate is exact by construction, non-agent pixels are 99.88-99.99% bit-identical, and that residual is the reference's multisampling bleeding agent colour into neighbouring wall/floor pixels. On agent-interior pixels (eroded by one, so coverage is not in play) the shader reaches 48-84% within 1/255 for pointmaze (mean 1.2-2.5/255), 0-51% for antmaze and 0-3% for humanoidmaze. The legged numbers are not a shading result: at the official whole-maze camera the ant is 12-70 px and the humanoid 4-21 px of 50176, and eroding leaves as few as 1 interior pixel, so their agreement is a statement about a silhouette under the reference's coverage rule. Against a NON-multisampled reference the same shader reaches 92.4% within 1/255 over 13,069 pooled agent-interior pixels (mean 0.88/255), which localises most of the remaining gap in MSAA sample positions -- GPU-specific, not recoverable by fitting -- rather than in shading. Three mechanisms were tried and are recorded so they are not retried: the fixed-function composition (FIXED - specular is additive and white and the texture modulates the whole primary colour, which took wall tops from 13/255 worst to 2/255 and made vertical wall faces bit-exact); Gouraud interpolation over MuJoCo's sphere/capsule tessellation (REFUTED - ten (slices, stacks, phase, diagonal) variants all scored 0.0-0.2% exact against 1.6% for plain per-pixel, which is also what sub-pixel triangles predict); and a mis-shaped specular lobe (REFUTED - a 24-knot piecewise-linear lobe fitted under four light/specular geometries, headlight directional or point crossed with Blinn or Phong, could not explain floor and agent with one curve).", "state_dim": 2, "default_size": 224, "state_names": ["x", "y"], "step_reference_exact": null, "step_reference_control": null, "step_reference_gate": null, "class": "PointmazeMediumNavigate", "module": "stable_envs.envs.ogbench", "doc": "OGBench pointmaze-medium-navigate. State is qpos (2 floats); the dataset suffix selects a task and does not reach the renderer, so it shares a baked plate with the other pointmaze-